  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong westerly winds on Tuesday could lead to \nsome isolated power outages. Also, the strong wind coupled with\nthe colder temperatures will lead to dangerous wind chills in \nthe mountains Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION... \nConfidence has increased for strong wind gusts Tuesday morning \nand afternoon as an area of low pressure deepens in the Northern\nMaritimes. Winds will begin to pick up Monday night and peak \nTuesday mid-morning through early afternoon. Winds will \ngradually weaken Tuesday evening into the overnight.\nProbabilities for 40+ mph wind gusts on Tuesday have increased \nto 70-90% for northern areas. As a result of the increased \nconfidence, a wind advisory has been issued on Tuesday for \nMillinocket, Greenville, and areas north. For the Bangor region \nand interior Downeast, 35+ mph wind gusts probabilities have \nincreased to 50-70%. It is likely that significant leaf loss\nwill occur in areas of Northern Maine where peak foliage has \nbeen reached. Additional leaf loss is possible in the Central \nHighlands to Downeast Maine where the foliage is moderate to \nhigh. Gusty winds could also lead to some isolated power \noutages.\n\nWith the colder airmass moving, the strong wind will lead to wind\nchills dangerously cold in the mountains Monday night through\nTuesday night. Katahdin will have wind chills in the teens \neven in the middle of the day Tuesday. Hikers should be \nprepared for winter conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A period of rain is expected today (Monday), mainly\nin Northern Maine. Totals of one tenth to one half inch are \nexpected in the north, with a tenth of an inch or less Downeast.\nLittle or no drought relief is expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nRain today is looking a bit more impressive than before for the\nnorth. That said, we still are only expecting from one tenth to\none half inch, not exactly much for drought relief. Areas \nDowneast will be lucky to get one tenth of an inch. The main \nperiod of rain in the north is from about 8am to 2pm. With the \ncold front moving through early afternoon, a thunderstorm or two\nare possible over the north. Drying is expected for mid to late\nafternoon behind the cold front, though an isolated shower \nstill cannot be ruled out.",
